The '''History of Sarta (1846–1906)''' covers the period from the return of the coastal territories in the [[Occidental Agreement of 1846]] to the victory of the [[Partido Nacional]] in the lower house in 1906. The era was shaped by industrial expansion, coastal urban growth, educational inequality between provinces, and a cautious liberalisation of the political order that left the lords' parliament dominant for most of the period. The crown remained the symbolic centre of the kingdom, but domestic affairs increasingly depended on the royal companies, provincial administration, and parliamentary bargaining.

== 1846–1855: industrial expansion and succession ==

The return of the coast through the [[Occidental Agreement of 1846]] gave Sarta a larger territorial base and a stronger industrial frontier. The restored lands of [[Occidental Province]] brought substantial population and commercial capacity back under Sartan rule, and they quickly became the main destination for migrants leaving the southern and central countryside, especially the [[Sierra Madre]].

The most visible beneficiaries of that movement were [[Bahia Alta]] and [[Mariapolis]]. Bahia Alta expanded rapidly as a port and processing centre, while Mariapolis remained the largest city in the kingdom. Growth also spread through the returned coast more broadly, where shipping, warehousing, and light industry expanded faster than in the interior. The coast had already been the kingdom's commercial axis, and the restored territory pulled even more investment and labour toward it.

The state responded to the new economic conditions by building a network of royal companies to manage public works and industry. Between 1846 and 1880, the crown chartered a series of semi-autonomous ''compañías reales'' for tasks ranging from roadway construction to railway construction. These firms operated with considerable day-to-day autonomy, but they remained answerable to parliament and were directly controlled by lords appointed through the upper house.

The most important of these companies was the [[Compañía Real de Minaria de Sarta]], formed in 1847 to manage coal mining operations. It became the only company with direct control over mining and quickly developed into a major pillar of the Sartan economy. Coal from the [[Sierra Madre]] fed both domestic industry and Volisanian demand, making the company profitable but also politically sensitive.

The company system gave the state a way to direct industrial growth without surrendering control to private capital. At the same time, it strengthened the influence of the upper-house elite, who used the royal companies to organise investment, employment, and infrastructure on their own terms.

== Industrial consolidation and landholding ==

The industrial boom did not distribute ownership evenly. Some firms were direct crown companies, while others were not formally crown property but were controlled by royal relatives, senior lords, or allied merchant houses through charters, board appointments, and land grants. In practice, the same narrow elite directed mines, roads, docks, and later railway concessions. Industry became a tool of state and family power rather than a broad private market.

Land ownership followed the same pattern. Returned coastal land, urban parcels, and estate land near the Sierra Madre were increasingly consolidated into large holdings tied to company founders and parliamentary families. Large tracts around ports and industrial corridors were set aside for docks, warehouses, worker housing, and depots, which sped up urban growth in Bahia Alta, Mariapolis, and the coastal strip. But it also pushed tenants, rural labourers, and smallholders into wage work and made them more dependent on the companies for housing and employment.

That dependence sharpened labour conflict. Wages, rents, and working conditions were set from above, and workers had few alternatives once the coast and the mines became the main centres of employment. The coal stoppages of 1854 were the first major sign of that tension, but they were part of a wider pattern that later reached port labour and railway construction.

The industrial boom also produced harsher conditions in the mines. Toxic air, dust, and unsafe work practices led to repeated stoppages in 1854, with more than fifteen interruptions in coal production over the course of the year. The protests were led by workers who were demanding safer conditions, compensation, and a greater voice in how the companies were managed.

On 8 April 1855, [[Alfonso II of Sarta]] died and [[Carlos II of Sarta]] succeeded him. The same year, Sarta introduced the [[House of Commons of Sarta]] as part of a cautious liberalisation of the political system. The new chamber was elected by universal suffrage for adult men of the Petran faith, giving the kingdom a wider electoral base without surrendering control to full democracy.

The reform was intended to channel industrial unrest into parliamentary politics. Rather than allowing worker grievances to remain outside the system, the state created another body through which votes could be counted and conflicts contained. The king remained a figurehead of stability with real influence, but the lords' parliament continued to manage domestic affairs.

== 1855–1883: coastal autonomy and schooling ==

Education remained highly uneven across the kingdom. In the restored coast, schooling was comparatively secure because [[Occidental Province]] retained its own systems of education, governance, and law for fifty years after the merger. The province kept local courts, school inspectors, and district administrators in place, so day-to-day life there changed more slowly than in the interior. It was not fully independent, but it functioned as a protected provincial bloc inside Sarta rather than as an ordinary district.

That arrangement made Occidental a magnet for families seeking guaranteed schooling and predictable administration, and it contributed to continuing migration toward the coast. The province's schools produced a larger literate public, while inland districts remained more dependent on parish instruction, private tutors, or irregular local provision. The result was a widening contrast between Occidental and the rest of the kingdom. The coast developed a more regular school system, a denser legal bureaucracy, and a stronger provincial identity than most inland regions.

Occidental's special status also affected politics. Provincial leaders could bargain with the crown from a stronger position because they represented a region that mattered to trade, shipping, and public revenue. Even after the province's separate systems were gradually folded into the national framework, the habits of administrative independence remained visible in the coast's institutions.

Industrial growth continued to draw people out of the countryside. Many rural households, especially in the southern Sierra Madre, sent family members to the coast in search of wages, while others moved permanently into the expanding cities. The result was a steady flow of workers toward Bahia Alta, the ports, and Mariapolis.

This movement changed the social balance of the kingdom. Coastal districts and the capital increasingly dominated industrial output, while the hinterlands lagged behind. The royal companies reinforced that trend by concentrating employment, transport, and investment in the same places where the population was already growing.

The imbalance eventually forced the state to act. In 1883, Sarta introduced national primary school education in order to reduce the gap and extend basic schooling beyond Occidental Province. The reform reflected both practical pressure and political competition: the kingdom could not ignore a coastal province that was attracting people partly because it already offered better schooling.

== Literacy and higher education ==

National primary schooling increased literacy steadily in the late nineteenth century, especially in [[Occidental Province]], [[Bahia Alta]], and [[Mariapolis]]. More children learned to read for parish records, newspapers, commercial correspondence, and company work. Clerks, teachers, shopkeepers, and junior civil servants formed a larger educated middle layer than Sarta had had earlier in the century.

In the same period, the crown and provincial authorities began organising a small university system to train lawyers, doctors, teachers, and administrators. It was concentrated in [[Mariapolis]] and [[Bahia Alta]], where government offices, courts, and commercial firms created steady demand for graduates. Access remained uneven, since tuition, relocation costs, and entrance preparation favoured coastal families and the sons of company managers, merchants, and officials, but the system helped produce a more literate bureaucracy and a more self-conscious urban public.

== 1883–1893: labour politics and foreign alignment ==

By the late nineteenth century, two broad political currents had become visible. The pro-business, pro-[[Volisania]] [[Liga Progressivo]] drew support from coastal merchants, company directors, and parts of the capital. It favoured closer commercial ties, weaker labour restrictions, and continued cooperation with Volisania.

Opposing it was the anti-Volisania, pro-worker [[Union del Personajes]], which drew strength from miners, rural communities, and inland districts that had borne the costs of industrial growth. It supported stronger labour protections and a more independent Sartan position. The split was clearest between the coast and the capital on one side, and the rural, mountainous, and interior districts on the other.

Public opinion in these years remained mixed. Volisanian propaganda and news circulated widely, but many people in Sarta were not strongly pro-Volisania, and some merchants and urban readers even viewed [[Zong]] as a counterweight to Volisanian power. By the early 1890s, the anti-Volisania parliamentary bloc still held the upper hand, and there was little appetite for sending Sartan forces into a war that many considered a foreign quarrel.

== 1893–1899: the Second War of the Isthmus ==

The [[Second War of the Isthmus]] broke out in 1893 between a Zong-led coalition and a Volisanian-led coalition defending [[Balboa]]. In Sarta, parliament initially treated participation as a waste of lives and money. The anti-Volisania parties were still influential, and the government did not want to commit regular forces to Volisania's side.

By 1894, however, the war was changing the political atmosphere. News of Volisania losing territory began to dominate public debate, and [[Sinchew]] forces moved through Balboa and toward the Aethiopean coast. That development stirred elite anxiety in Sarta over the weakness of Illypnian powers and the possibility that Volisania might lose the isthmus entirely.

The government still hesitated to send a formal expedition, but in 1895 it authorised a volunteer force of 8,000 men. That contingent was a compromise: it allowed Sarta to show support for Volisania without making the war look like a full national commitment. The move also reflected pressure from sectors of the elite who feared that a decisive Volisanian defeat would weaken Illypnia as a whole.

The actual war was hard to defend at home. Volisanian propaganda had not produced strong enthusiasm in Sarta, and the losses on the isthmus were increasingly blamed on the Union del Personajes and its allies. The fighting and the surrounding news cycle made the conflict into a domestic argument about loyalty, foreign dependence, and the costs of parliamentary caution.

== 1899–1906: backlash and the National Party ==

The end of the war did not resolve the political dispute. Instead, the Volisanian defeat fed a backlash against the Union del Personajes and the anti-war parliamentary majority. A new nationalist current gathered around [[Jaime Checa]], a volunteer fighter who returned from the war aligned with the [[National Volisan Party|National Volisan Party (partit nacional volisà)]] and used that position to build a wider political base.

The war's end also helped lead to the global [[Second War of the Isthmus#The Mild Depression|Mild Depression]]. Volisanian imports collapsed once wartime demand fell away, leaving coal surpluses in the Sierra Madre and forcing companies to cut back production. Layoffs spread through the mines, rail links, docks, and associated workshops, and unemployment rose sharply in the coastal cities and company towns. The downturn made industrial workers more restive and gave nationalist organisers a wider audience among displaced labourers and frustrated smallholders.

Checa's movement became the [[Partido Nacional]], which attacked the Union del Personajes for weakness during the war and for failing to defend Sarta's place in Illypnian politics. The party also benefited from continuing labour unrest. From the late 1880s onward, Sarta had seen repeated strikes and protests, and the atmosphere remained unsettled in the years after the war.

The crisis deepened when the [[Darasian Socialist Revolution]] of 1904–1905 emboldened local revolutionaries and frightened conservative voters. National Party organisers presented themselves as the answer to both socialist agitation and parliamentary paralysis. Their message was especially effective among voters who associated the Union del Personajes with hesitation, disorder, and foreign humiliation.

In 1906, the [[Partido Nacional]] won a majority of seats in the lower house. That victory closed the era that had begun with the return of the coast. Sarta entered a new political phase in which nationalism, anti-socialism, and postwar reaction would dominate parliamentary politics.

== Aftermath ==

By 1906, Sarta had become more urban, more industrial, and more politically divided than it had been in 1846. The coast had consolidated its lead, Bahia Alta had emerged as a major industrial city, the royal companies had become central to the economy, and the state had been forced to expand basic education beyond Occidental Province.

The era left Sarta with a more centralised industrial economy, a larger urban population, and a political system still dominated by elites but no longer entirely closed. It also left a deeper contrast between the provinces that had long benefited from guaranteed institutions and those that had not.

